    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **29 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the city-capacity rule for creating owners: a `POST /api/owners` is now rejected with **409** when the request's city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the existing OfficeFloor function-injection patterns in this repo (mirroring the sibling `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique` / `CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique` checks), I added:\n\n1. **`CheckOwnerCityCapacity`** (function) \u2014 reads the request via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, counts owners whose city matches case-insensitively (consistent with `AssignCustomerCode`'s per-city counting), and throws `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is `>= 50`. It runs before the owner is saved, so the count excludes the owner being created.\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException`** \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`) mapped to 409.\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT` with `{\"errors\": [\"city\"]}`, matching the shape of the other duplicate-check handlers.\n4. **`...CityAtCapacityException.yml`** \u2014 escalation wiring for the handler.\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `checkCityCapacity` step (under `govern: [ transaction ]`) after `checkHouseholdUnique` and before `build`.\n\nThe dedicated acceptance test `Cp18Tests.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fills a fresh city to exactly 50, expects the 51st to be a 409) passes, and no existing tests regressed.",
